===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{term-label|en|formal}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[solemnly]] [[reject#Verb|reject]] (someone or something); to [[abandon#Verb|abandon]] (someone or something) [[forever#Adverb|forever]]; to [[disavow]], to [[disclaim]], to [[repudiate]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|renounce|Thesaurus:repudiate}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''abjure''' errors}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|lines=1115–1117|sig=Dd2|verso=1|page=213|passage=She diſappeerd, and left me dark, I vvak'd / To find her, or for ever to deplore / Her loſs, and other pleaſures all '''abjure''': {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Macaulay Edinburgh Review|volume=I|page=32|passage=[B]oth [[w:Charles I of England|Charles {{quote-gloss|I}}]] himself and his creature [[w:William Laud|{{quote-gloss|William}} Laud]], while they '''abjured''' the innocent badges of Popery, retained all its worst vices, {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1902|author=[[w:Robert Marshall (dramatist)|Robert Marshall]]|chapter=About Myself|title=The Haunted Major|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Grant & Murray{{nb...|Ltd., 126 Princes Street, Edinburgh.}}|year_published=1932|page=17|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/hauntedmajor0000robe/page/17/mode/1up|oclc=752709593|passage=[S]he is an ardent golfer. Except during the season in town, she spends her year in golfing, either at St. Magnus or Pau, for, like all good Americans, she has long since '''abjured''' her native soil.}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} To [[renounce#Verb|renounce]] (something) upon [[oath#Noun|oath]]; to [[forswear]]; specifically, to [[recant]] or [[retract#Verb|retract]] (a [[heresy]] or some other [[opinion#Noun|opinion]]); to [[withdraw#Verb|withdraw]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##: {{synonyms|en|disavow|renounce|Thesaurus:recant}}
##: {{ux|en|to '''abjure''' allegiance to a prince}}
##* {{RQ:Thomas More Workes|title=Heresyes|book=I|chapter=I|page=108|column=2|passage=[T]he man ye write of, was of many thinges borne wrõge {{quote-gloss|wrong}} in hande, & therin ſo ſore handeled that he was forced to for ſwere and '''abiure''' certaine hereſyes, & openly to penance therfore, where he neuer held any ſuch.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|act=V|scene=i|page=16|column=2|passage=Graues at my command, / Haue vvak'd their ſleepers, op'd, and let 'em forth / By my ſo potent Art. But this rough Magicke / I heere '''abiure''': {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Burke American Taxation|page=36|passage=[T]he conceſſion vvas far ſhort of ſatisfying the principle vvhich Lord Hillſborough had '''abjured'''; or even the pretence on vvhich the repeal of the other taxes vvas announced: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Beckford Vathek|page=39|passage=VVouldeſt thou devote thyſelf to me? Adore then the terreſtrial influences; and '''abjure''' [[w:Muhammad|Mahomet]].}}
##* {{RQ:O'Keeffe Zenobia|volume=II|page=247|passage=[W]hen, guided by learned rabbis, invincible generals, and sublime and enlightened heathens, thou in evil hour '''abjuredst''' the gospel, in outward act if not in heart, and ungratefully dismissed thy faithful adviser, thou didst seal the fate of thy country and thyself.}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} To [[cause#Verb|cause]] (someone) to recant or retract (a heresy or some other opinion). {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
##* {{RQ:Fuller Worthies of England|part=1|chapter=Bark-shire|page=90|passage=[T]hey vvere betrayed, and then many of them to the number of ſix or ſeven ſcore vvere '''abjured''', and three or four of them burnt. Novv although vve knevv not hovv to call theſe Martyrs vvho ſo ſuffered, ''their Names'' no doubt ''are vvritten in the Book of Life''.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|law|historical}}
### ''Especially in'' '''[[abjure the realm]]''': to [[swear#Verb|swear]] an oath to [[leave#Verb|leave]] (a [[place#Noun|place]]) forever.
### {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} To cause or [[compel]] (someone) to leave a place forever; to [[banish]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## To solemnly reject; to abandon forever.
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|lines=470–473|page=26|passage=Eaſily canſt thou find one miſerable, / And not inforc'd oft-times to part from truth; / If it may ſtand him more in ſtead to lye, / Say and unſay, feign, flatter, or '''abjure'''?}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} To recant or retract a heresy on oath.
##* {{RQ:Thomas More Workes|title=Heresyes|book=IV|chapter=XVI|page=282|column=1|passage=Nor neuer yet found I ani. j. but he would once '''abiure''', though he neuer intended to kepe his [[oath|othe]].}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Umberto Eco|chapter=|translator=w:William Weaver|title=w:The Name of the Rose|series=A [[w:Helen Wolff|Helen Wolff]] and [[w:Kurt Wolff (publisher)|Kurt Wolff]] Book|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harcourt (publisher)|Harcourt Brace Jovanovich]]|year=1983|page=238|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/nameofrose000ecou/page/238/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-15-144647-6|passage=And going toward the Justice Gate, many said to him, "Recant! Recant! Don't insist on dying," and he said, "Christ died for us." {{...}} At the Field of Justice, one said to him he should do as a certain monk, his superior, had done, '''abjuring''': but Michael answered that he would not '''abjure''', and I saw many in the crowd agree and urge Michael to be strong: so I and many others realized those were his followers, and we moved away from them.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|law|historical}} To swear an oath to leave a place forever.

